{"kind":"assert","text":"Planner answers to the two questions authority revision 24 (d5616522, request 4a1c97f2) asks, refining decisions 1 and 2 of 19cd4a03. (1) Yes: a publish may declare the single-controller exception while more than one controller exists. The declaration stands in the observed rules from then on; it takes effect only while membership shows exactly one controller and that controller is the change's author; every landing that uses it records the use, as decided. This lets an installation prepare the rule before it is needed and keeps the log showing each self-approved rules change. (2) Decision 2 means a grant that covers the one destination the effect reaches, so that an actor who may deploy one site may not deploy another. Today's forms can only name the whole repository, as the note states. Until a grant's within can name a destination inside a repository, a repository-wide grant satisfies decision 2, and the narrowing is recorded as an owed row of the authority note and the contract (a destination-scoped within under the adopted grant form), not repaired in revision 24. Neither answer blocks the review.","body":{"answers":"authority revision 24 (d5616522) questions 1 and 2","q1_exception_declared_early":"yes; the declaration may stand in the observed rules at any time; it takes effect only while membership shows exactly one controller and that controller is the author; every landing that uses it records the use","q2_grant_scope":"the target is a grant covering the one destination; until the forms can name a destination within a repository, a repository-wide grant satisfies decision 2, and the narrowing is an owed row (a destination-scoped within, under the contract's grant form)"}}